# NAME

sbank-deposit - deposit CPU or GPU-hour credits to a slurm account

# SYNOPSIS

sbank deposit [OPTION]...

sbank deduct [OPTION]...

# DESCRIPTION

slurm bank administrative tool for adding or removing finite resource-hour
credits on a Slurm account. Deposits accept positive whole hours and, for
backward compatibility, zero as a successful no-op. Deductions require positive
whole hours. CPU is the default resource. GPU-hour banking is selected with
`--tres gres/gpu`.

CPU credits continue to use the legacy `GrpCPUMins` association limit for
compatibility with existing slurm-bank installations.

GPU credits use the `gres/gpu` entry in Slurm's `GrpTRESMins`. `GrpTRESMins`
is a cumulative limit against the effective TRES usage maintained by the
priority/multifactor plugin for the account and its children.

An unset `gres/gpu` limit means that this account association has no local
GPU-minute limit; it does not mean a zero balance. Ancestor associations, user
associations, QOS or partition policy can still impose independent limits. On
the first GPU deposit, sbank initializes a finite account limit at the account's
current effective GPU usage, plus GPU minutes committed to currently running
jobs, a small precision reserve, and the deposited credit. This makes the
requested first deposit available relative to this account-level bank rather
than retroactively charging work that ran before the bank was initialized.

GPU deductions are limited to withdrawable credit. sbank reads the live
`GrpTRESRaw` and `TRESRunMins` state from `sshare` and refuses to lower the
limit into effective usage or time already committed to running jobs. Slurm
21.08 reports those two aggregate values in whole minutes, so sbank keeps a
conservative two-minute reserve for sub-minute truncation. During first-time
initialization this reserve is added to the configured limit; consequently the
Slurm limit can contain up to two minutes of administrative cushion beyond the
whole-hour deposit. sbank does not treat that cushion as withdrawable credit.

# GPU ACCOUNTING REQUIREMENTS

For generic GPU accounting the target cluster must track `gres/gpu`, normally
by including:

    AccountingStorageTRES=gres/gpu

`GrpTRESMins` enforcement requires:

    PriorityType=priority/multifactor

GPU banking requires `AccountingStorageEnforce` to include `limits` (the
`safe` and `all` settings also qualify). Slurm 21.08 maintains the running
TRES-minute commitments used by sbank when limits are enforced.
`AccountingStorageEnforce=safe` is recommended for bank-style operation because
it prevents a job from starting against a `GrpTRESMins` limit unless Slurm
expects the job can run to completion. With only `limits`, a job can start and
later be killed when its TRES-minute limit is reached.

The accounting period must be chosen deliberately. Association TRES usage is
subject to `PriorityDecayHalfLife` and `PriorityUsageResetPeriod`. A decay or
reset replenishes effective balance without changing the configured
`GrpTRESMins` limit. Slurm 21.08 also warns that a zero decay half-life with no
reset period can eventually roll over its priority usage counters. Therefore
there is no universally correct permanent-bank setting: configure decay/reset
for the site's accounting period and understand that those settings define
when credits effectively replenish.

A job QOS `UsageFactor` also scales association TRES usage in Slurm's
multifactor accounting. If all applicable QOS values use `UsageFactor=1`, one
banked `gres/gpu` hour corresponds to one allocated generic GPU for one hour.
With another UsageFactor, the bank is measured in Slurm's weighted effective
GPU-hours instead.

Generic `gres/gpu` counts configured GPU GRES units; it is not a measurement of
GPU utilization. Four GPUs allocated for two hours normally consume eight
GPU-hours even when the application leaves GPUs idle. Sites with heterogeneous
GPUs or MIG instances should also decide whether one generic GRES unit is a
suitable common accounting unit. This command intentionally does not assign
different economic weights to GPU models or MIG profiles.

The root association is rejected for GPU banking because Slurm does not
enforce `GrpTRESMins` on the root association. Other association, QOS and
partition limits continue to apply independently and can make less capacity
schedulable than the account-level bank alone would suggest.

# CONCURRENT ADMINISTRATION

Slurm 21.08 has no atomic `GrpTRESMins += value` operation. sbank must read the
current limit, calculate a replacement value, and write it. deposit and deduct
therefore take an advisory `flock` on the account before changing either CPU or
GPU limits.

The default lock directory is:

    /var/lock/slurm-bank

Set `SLURMBANK_LOCK_DIR` to another writable directory when necessary. If
sbank can be run from more than one administration host, that directory must be
on a filesystem whose `flock` locks are shared by all of those hosts. Direct
`sacctmgr` changes made without taking the same lock can still race with sbank
and should be operationally serialized.

The advisory lock cannot make Slurm 21.08 transactional across slurmdbd and
slurmctld. A job can theoretically start after sbank's final live-state check
and before a lowered limit reaches the controller, and first-time conversion
from an unlimited account has the same narrow scheduling race. sbank refreshes
`sshare` immediately before a deduction or initialization and recommends
`AccountingStorageEnforce=safe`, but sites requiring a strict no-race guarantee
must also prevent new jobs for that account while changing or initializing its
GPU bank.

# OPTIONS

* -h

  show help message

* -d

  show debug messages

* -a, --account

  Specify the Slurm account.

* -c, --cluster

  Specify the Slurm cluster.

* -t, --time

  Specify whole hours to deposit or deduct. A deposit of zero is accepted as
  a successful no-op for backward compatibility; deductions must be greater
  than zero. Non-zero values must be canonical base-10 integers without leading
  zeroes and must fit within sbank's safe integer range.

* -r, --tres

  Select the resource. Supported values are `cpu` (default) and `gres/gpu`.

# EXAMPLES

Deposit 1000000 CPU-hours:

    sbank deposit -c mycluster -a account-00049 -t 1000000

Deposit 1000 generic GPU-hours:

    sbank deposit -c mycluster -a gpu-project -t 1000 -r gres/gpu

Remove 250 currently withdrawable GPU-hours:

    sbank deduct -c mycluster -a gpu-project -t 250 -r gres/gpu

Inspect the live multifactor state used for GPU-limit decisions:

    sshare -M mycluster -A gpu-project -l -o Account,User,GrpTRESMins,GrpTRESRaw,TRESRunMins

`sreport -T gres/gpu` remains useful for historical database reporting, but a
reporting interval is not the same thing as the live balance enforced by the
priority plugin.

